main\n    t()\n  File \"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__bugfix__r2__1791297484/work/run_tests.py\", line 31, in test_parse_date\n    assert parse_date(\"2026-10-06\") == (2026, 10, 6)\nAssertionError\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__bugfix__r2__1791297484/work/run_tests.py\", line 54, in main\n    t()\n  File \"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__bugfix__r2__1791297484/work/run_tests.py\", line 36, in test_parse_record\n    assert rec == {\"date\": (2026, 10, 6), \"item\": \"widget\", \"qty\": 3, \"unit_cents\": 450}\nAssertionError\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__bugfix__r2__1791297484/work/run_tests.py\", line 54, in main\n    t()\n  File \"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__bugfix__r2__1791297484/work/run_tests.py\", line 46, in test_summary_line\n    assert summary_line(recs, 3) == \"widget=$13.50; gadget=$4.00; gizmo=$2.50\"\nAssertionError\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__bugfix__r2__1791297484/work/run_tests.py\", line 54, in main\n    t()\n  File \"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-kalepail-Desktop-kalepail-skills/fd5aae97-76bc-4bb3-8597-af0fa91bb3f0/scratchpad/probe-runs/glmflash-max__bugfix__r2__1791297484/work/run_tests.py\", line 41, in test_top_items\n    assert top_items(recs, 2) == [(\"widget\", 1350), (\"gadget\", 400)]\nAssertionError\nFAIL test_format_cents\nPASS test_parse_amount\nFAIL test_parse_date\nFAIL test_parse_record\nFAIL test_summary_line\nFAIL test_top_items\n1/6 passed\n","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 run_tests.py","time":{"start":1791297586683,"end":1791297586773}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me run the tests first to see what fails.\n\nLet me analyze the code against the docstrings and visible tests:\n\n**money.py:**\n- `parse_amount(\"$1,234.56\")` should be 123456. Let's trace: s=\"$1,234.56\", neg=False, s = s.lstrip(\"-\").lstrip(\"$\").replace(\",\", \"\"). `lstrip(\"-\")` strips leading dashes → \"$1,234.56\", `lstrip(\"$\")` → \"1,234.56\", replace commas → \"1234.56\". Split → whole=\"1234\", frac=\"56\" → (frac+\"00\")[:2] = \"5600\"? Wait: \"56\" + \"00\" = \"5600\", [:2] = \"56\". OK that's right. cents = 1234*100 + 56 = 123456. ✓\n- `parse_amount(\"-$3.10\")`: neg=True, s = lstrip(\"-\") → \"$3.10\", lstrip(\"$\") → \"3.10\", cents = 310, return -310. ✓\n- `parse_amount(\"12\")`: whole=\"12\", frac=\"00\" → 1200. ✓\n- `parse_amount(\"0.5\")` per docstring: whole=\"0\", frac=\"5\" → (frac+\"00\")[:2] = \"500\"[:2]=\"50\" → 0*100+50 = 50. Hmm, \"0.5\" means 50 cents. Correct. But wait — what about \"0.50\"? frac=\"50\" → \"5000\"[:2] = \"50\" → 50. ✓. What about \"0.05\"? frac=\"05\" → \"0500\"[:2] = \"05\" → int(\"05\") = 5. ✓. What about \"$1.5\"? frac=\"5\" → 50 → 150. ✓.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtle bug: `int(whole or \"0\")` — if s is \"\" (e.g., \"$\" alone)? Not necessarily a hidden test.\n\nActually wait — there's a real bug with negative handling: `s.lstrip(\"-\")` only strips leading dashes, which is fine for \"-$3.10\". But what about \"$-3.10\"? lstrip(\"-\") first does nothing (\"$...\" doesn't start with \"-\"), then lstrip(\"$\") → \"-3.10\", then \".\" in s, split(\".\") → whole=\"-3\"... then int(\"-3\") works → -300+10 = -290? Hmm, neg is False so returns -290. Is that a documented behavior? Not specified. Edge case.\n\nAlso negative with format: `format_cents(-310)`: sign=\"-\", cents=-310, `cents // 100` → -310 // 100 = -4 (floor division!), `-4` formatted with `,` → \"-4\", and `cents % 100` → -310 % 100 = 90 → \"90\". So result is \"--$-4.90\"? Wait: f\"{sign}${cents // 100:,}.{cents % 100:02d}\" = \"-\" + \"$\" + \"-4\" + \".\" + \"90\" = \"-$-4.90\". Expected \"-$3.10\". So format_cents is buggy for negatives. Root cause: floor division on negative numbers. Fix: use abs value.\n\n`format_cents(123456)`: 123456//100 = 1234, %100 = 56 → \"$1,234.56\". ✓\n\nSo bug 1: `format_cents` with negative cents — must normalize with abs.\n\n**parse.py:**\n- `parse_date(\"2026-10-06\")` should return (2026, 10, 6). Current: `y, m, d = text.strip().split(\"-\")` then `(int(y), int(d), int(m))` — swapped month and day! Returns (2026, 6, 10). Bug 2: swapped m/d in return.\n\n- `parse_record`: `[p.strip() for p in line.split(\"|\")]` — splits on \"|\" without maxsplit. If the item name contained \"|\" it'd break, but more importantly, the docstring example has 4 fields. Hmm, but a subtle bug: line.split(\"|\") for a line with exactly 4 parts is fine. Wait, what about extra whitespace? Strip handles that. Item lowercased ✓. But wait — is there a bug? `date_s, item, qty, price = [p.strip() for p in line.split(\"|\")]` — if a line has more than 4 fields (e.g., item with | in it), unpacking fails. Not documented. Hmm.\n\nWait, actually let me re-check parse_record against the test: \"2026-10-06 | Widget | 3 | $4.50\" → {'date': (2026, 10, 6), 'item': 'widget', 'qty': 3, 'unit_cents': 450}. With the m/d swap fixed, parse_date returns (2026, 10, 6) ✓. item \"Widget\".lower() = \"widget\" ✓. qty 3 ✓. parse_amount(\"$4.50\") = 450 ✓.\n\nHmm, but there might be a bug with item not being stripped? `[p.strip() for p in ...]` strips all parts, then `item.lower()`. Fine.\n\n- `parse_ledger`: skips blank lines and lines starting with '#'. `line.lstrip().startswith(\"#\")` — handles leading whitespace before #. ✓. Hmm wait — the docstring says \"Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are skipped\". The header line in SAMPLE is \"# date | item | qty | price\" — starts with #, skipped ✓.\n\nHmm, wait — is there a bug in parse_ledger? Let me check `line.strip()` — `if not line.strip() or ...`. Fine.\n\n**report.py:**\n- `top_items` should return largest totals first (descending), ties broken by item name ascending. Current sorts ascending by total only, then takes `[:n]` — gives smallest. Bug 3: sort direction. Fix: sort by (-total, item) descending, take n.\n\nTest: totals: widget: 3*450 + 0*450 = 1350; gadget: 400; gizmo: 250. top 2 → [(\"widget\", 1350), (\"gadget\", 400)]. With fix: sorted by (-total, item) → widget 1350, gadget 400, gizmo 250 → [widget, gadget][:2] ✓.\n\n- `summary_line(recs, 3)` → \"widget=$13.50; gadget=$4.00; gizmo=$2.50\". With fixed top_items and format_cents (all positive here), it works: format_cents(1350) → \"$13.50\" ✓, 400 → \"$4.00\" ✓, 250 → \"$2.50\" ✓.\n\nWait — does format_cents(400) give \"$4.00\"? 400//100 = 4, 400%100 = 0 → \"$4.00\" ✓.\n\nNow let me check for more subtle bugs that hidden tests might catch, matching docstrings:\n\n1. `parse_amount`: docstring says parse '$1,234.56', '-$3.10', '12' or '0.5'. Potential issues:\n   - \"-$3.10\" handled. What about \"$-3.10\"? Not documented.\n   - Negative decimals: \"-$3.10\" → -310 ✓.\n   - What about \"-310\"? neg=True, lstrip(\"-\") → \"310\" → -31000? Wait \"310\" has no dot → whole=\"310\", frac=\"00\" → 31000, return -31000. Hmm that's -310.00 dollars = -31000 cents ✓.\n   - What about \"1,234.5\"? frac=\"5\" → 50 → 123450 ✓.\n   - What about trailing text? Not documented.\n   - What about \"$1,234.567\"? frac=\"567\" → (frac+\"00\")[:2] = \"5600\"[:2] = \"56\" — truncates to 56 cents. Docstring doesn't specify rounding. Truncation is reasonable.\n   - Hmm, wait: what about \"0.5\" → 50 ✓.\n   - What about \".5\"? \".\" in s → whole=\"\", frac=\"5\" → int(\"\" or \"0\") = 0, + 50 = 50 ✓. Actually `whole or \"0\"` handles empty whole ✓.\n   - What about \"12.\"? frac=\"\" → (\"00\")[:2] = \"00\" → 1200 ✓.\n   \n   Potential subtle bug: negative sign after \"$\": \"$-3.10\". lstrip(\"-\") doesn't strip (starts with $), lstrip(\"$\") → \"-3.10\", replace(\",\", \"\") → \"-3.10\". split(\".\") → whole=\"-3\" → int → -3*100 + 10 = -290, neg=False → -290. Wrong. But is \"$-3.10\" documented? No. The docstring says '-$3.10'. Hmm, hidden tests \"check the documented behaviour beyond the visible tests\" — so they check documented behavior. \"-$3.10\" is documented and works.\n\n   But wait — actually, let me reconsider. A cleaner fix: handle the sign after stripping \"$\" too. But careful not to break documented behavior. Let me think about what \"root causes\" are. The task says \"Find and fix the bugs in the package so the behaviour matches the docstrings.\" So I should focus on documented behavior.\n\n   Documented behaviors:\n   - parse_amount: '$1,234.56', '-$3.10', '12', '0.5' → integer cents. ✓ works currently except... let me trace \"$1,234.56\" again carefully. Actually wait — I need to double check `lstrip` semantics: `s.lstrip(\"-\")` removes leading \"-\" chars. \"-$3.10\" → lstrip(\"-\") → \"$3.10\". Then `.lstrip(\"$\")` → \"3.10\". OK.\n   \n   Hmm, but actually there IS a bug with parse_amount and negatives combined with the comma: \"-$1,234.56\" → lstrip(\"-\") → \"$1,234.56\" → ... → -123456 ✓.\n\n   What about parse_amount(\"-3.10\")? → -310 ✓.\n\n   Hmm OK, so what are the actual bugs? Let me run the tests to see.\n\n2. `format_cents`: docstring says negative values look like '-$0.05'. Bug with negatives confirmed.\n\n3. `parse_date`: m/d swap confirmed.\n\n4. `parse_record`: item lower-cased and stripped ✓. Hmm — wait, is there a bug where item isn't stripped? No, it is.\n\n5. `parse_ledger`: skip blanks and #. ✓. Wait — what about lines that fail to parse? Not documented.\n\n6. `top_items`: descending + tie-break ascending. Bug confirmed.\n\n7. `summary_line`: uses top_items ✓.\n\n8. `line_total` / `totals_by_item`: fine.\n\nLet me also think about hidden-test-worthy edge cases in documented behavior:\n\n- `format_cents(-310)` → \"-$3.10\" (documented via test; docstring says negative values look like '-$0.05'). Fix with abs.\n- `parse_date` docstring: 'YYYY-MM-DD' → (y, m, d). Fix swap.\n- `top_items` docstring: largest first, ties by name ascending. Fix sort.\n- `parse_amount` docstring: '0.5' → 50. Works. But hmm, what about '0.05'? → 5 ✓. What about '-0.5'? → -50 ✓ (neg=True, s=\"0.5\" → 50 → -50 ✓).\n\nWait, actually let me re-check: \"-0.5\" → lstrip(\"-\") → \"0.5\" → 50 → -50 ✓.\n\n- What about parse_amount(\"1.5\") → 150 ✓.\n\nHmm, is there a bug in parse_amount at all? Let me think about `$1,234.56` more carefully — `.replace(\",\", \"\")` handles commas anywhere ✓.\n\nWhat about int(frac) when frac has fewer than 2 chars... \"5\" → \"500\"[:2] = \"50\" ✓. When frac is \"5\" and there are more than 2 chars \"567\" → \"5600\"[:2] = \"56\" ✓ truncation.\n\nHmm wait, actually there might be a subtle bug: `frac = (frac + \"00\")[:2]` — for frac=\"5\", result is \"50\" ✓ correct. For frac=\"\" → \"00\" ✓. OK.\n\nSo parse_amount seems fine for documented cases. But hold on — what about negative zero, or \"$0.5\"? → 50 ✓.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: is there a bug with `-` handling when the string is like \"-12\"? neg=True, s = \"12\" → 1200 → -1200 ✓ (that's -$12.00 in cents: -1200). Correct.\n\nOK let me also check parse_record more: what if qty has spaces — stripped ✓. int(qty) — what about \"+3\"? int handles ✓.\n\nWhat about the split with maxsplit: `line.split(\"|\")` — if a line has exactly 4 fields ✓. If item contains \"|\" it breaks, but that's not documented. Hmm, \"Do not modify run_tests.py\" and \"extra hidden tests will check the documented behaviour\" — so documented behavior only. The docstring: \"Parse one ledger line into a dict.\" Example given. Item names lower-cased and stripped.\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me look at parse_record again:\n\n```python\ndate_s, item, qty, price = [p.strip() for p in line.split(\"|\")]\n```\n\nIf a line has MORE than 4 pipe-separated fields, this raises ValueError. Not documented. If FEWER than 4, also raises. Not documented either. I'll leave it.\n\nBut hmm — what about `item.lower()` — docstring says \"Item names are lower-cased and stripped.\" Stripping happens via the list comprehension ✓. But what if the line is like \"2026-10-06 | Widget | 3 | $4.50 \" with trailing spaces — stripped ✓.\n\nNow `parse_ledger` — hmm, wait. Is there a bug here? Let me re-read:\n\n```python\nfor line in text.splitlines():\n    if not line.strip() or line.lstrip().startswith(\"#\"):\n        continue\n    out.append(parse_record(line))\n```\n\nDocstring: \"Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are skipped.\" ✓ handles whitespace-prefixed # too. Fine.\n\nNow `top_items`:\n\n```python\nranked = sorted(totals_by_item(records).items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1])\nreturn ranked[:n]\n```\n\nBugs: ascending sort (should be descending), no tie-break by name. Fix: `sorted(..., key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))` and return `ranked[:n]`. That gives descending total, ties broken by name ascending. ✓\n\n`summary_line` — uses top_items, so fixed indirectly. ✓\n\n`format_cents` fix:\n\n```python\ndef format_cents(cents):\n    sign = \"-\" if cents < 0 else \"\"\n    cents = abs(cents)\n    return f\"{sign}${cents // 100:,}.{cents % 100:02d}\"\n```\n\nCheck: -310 → sign \"-\", abs 310 → \"$3.10\" → \"-$3.10\" ✓. -5 → \"-$0.05\" ✓ (docstring example). 123456 → \"$1,234.56\" ✓.\n\n`parse_date` fix:\n\n```python\ny, m, d = text.strip().split(\"-\")\nreturn (int(y), int(m), int(d))\n```\n\nCheck: \"2026-10-06\" → (2026, 10, 6) ✓.\n\nNow, are there other bugs that hidden tests may catch based on docstrings? Let me think harder about parse_amount. The docstring says: \"Parse '$1,234.56', '-$3.10', '12' or '0.5' into integer cents.\"\n\nPotential hidden test: parse_amount(\"0.5\") == 50 ✓ works. parse_amount(\"$0.05\") == 5 ✓. parse_amount(\"-$0.05\") == -5 ✓. parse_amount(\"1,234\") == 123400 ✓. parse_amount(\"-1,234.56\") == -123456 ✓.\n\nHmm, what about \"$-3.10\"? Not documented... but a robust parser might handle it. The instruction says fix root causes so behavior matches docstrings. Hidden tests check documented behavior. \"-$3.10\" is the documented negative form. I could make it more robust to also accept \"$-3.10\" without breaking anything. Let me consider: strip sign anywhere among leading \"-\"/\"$\" chars:\n\n```python\ns = text.strip()\nneg = s.startswith(\"-\") or s.startswith(\"$-\") ... \n```\n\nSimplest robust approach: after stripping whitespace, remove a leading sign:\n\n```python\nneg = False\nif s and s[0] in \"-$\":\n    neg = s[0] == \"-\"\n    s = s[1:]\nif s.startswith(\"-\"):\n    neg = True\n    s = s[1:]\ns = s.lstrip(\"$\").replace(\",\", \"\")\n```\n\nHmm, that's adding complexity for an undocumented case. The task says \"Fix the root causes\" — the root causes are the actual bugs. I shouldn't over-engineer. But also hidden tests \"check the documented behaviour beyond the visible tests\" — beyond the VISIBLE tests, i.e., other documented behaviors. So: docstring examples and stated rules. Let me enumerate all stated rules:\n\nmoney.py:\n- \"All amounts are integer cents.\"\n- parse_amount: '$1,234.56', '-$3.10', '12', '0.5' → integer cents. (These exact forms work.)\n- format_cents: \"Format integer cents as '$1,234.56'. Negative values look like '-$0.05'.\" — bug for negatives → FIX.\n\nparse.py:\n- parse_date: 'YYYY-MM-DD' → (year, month, day) ints. — swap bug → FIX.\n- parse_record: example + \"Item names are lower-cased and stripped.\" — works.\n- parse_ledger: \"Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are skipped.\" — works.\n\nreport.py:\n- line_total: qty * unit_cents.\n- totals_by_item: {item: total_cents} summed.\n- top_items: n items largest total first, ties by item name ascending. — bug → FIX.\n- summary_line: 'widget=$13.50; gadget=$4.00' for top n. — fixed via top_items.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me double-check parse_record once more for a subtle bug. The test:\n\n```python\nrec = parse_record(\"2026-10-06 | Widget | 3 | $4.50\")\nassert rec == {\"date\": (2026, 10, 6), \"item\": \"widget\", \"qty\": 3, \"unit_cents\": 450}\n```\n\nWith fixes: date (2026,10,6) ✓, item \"widget\" ✓, qty 3 ✓, unit_cents 450 ✓.\n\nHmm, wait — what about item stripping: the comprehension strips each part, so \" Widget \" → \"Widget\" → lower \"widget\" ✓.\n\nNow let me also reconsider parse_amount for a potential hidden-test on trailing/leading whitespace: \" $4.50 \" → strip ✓.\n\nWhat about \"0.5\" with negative: \"-$0.5\" → -50 ✓.\n\nOK, now let me also think: is there possibly a bug in parse_amount with the fraction when whole is empty and there's a comma like \",5\"? Unlikely.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider the `frac = (frac + \"00\")[:2]` logic for a potential documented case \"0.5\" → 50. Yes correct.\n\nWhat about \"12\" → 1200 ✓.\n\nLet me now also consider `parse_amount` rounding: \".99\" → 99 ✓.\n\nHmm, one more potential issue: parse_amount with a bare \"$\" or \"\" → int(\"\") or \"0\" handles whole, frac \"00\" → 0. Fine.\n\nLet me now run the tests to confirm which fail currently, then apply fixes.\n\nActually wait, one more careful look at `top_items` ties. With key (-total, item), ties are broken by item name ascending ✓. E.g., two items both 400 → alphabetical. Good.\n\nAnd `summary_line` default n=3 ✓.\n\nAlso `line_total(rec)` — used by totals_by_item ✓.\n\nNow — are there any other subtle bugs? Let me re-read money.py parse_amount once more very carefully:\n\n```python\ns = text.strip()\nneg = s.startswith(\"-\")\ns = s.lstrip(\"-\").lstrip(\"$\").replace(\",\", \"\")\n```\n\nHmm, `lstrip(\"-\")` then `lstrip(\"$\")` — for \"$1,234.56\": lstrip(\"-\") → \"$1,234.56\" (no change), lstrip(\"$\") → \"1,234.56\" ✓.\n\nFor \"-$3.10\": → \"$3.10\" → \"3.10\" ✓.\n\nEdge: \"--5\"? lstrip strips all leading dashes → \"5\", neg True → -500. Fine-ish.\n\nEdge: \"$-5\"? → lstrip(\"-\") no-op → lstrip(\"$\") → \"-5\" → whole=\"-5\" → -500 + 0 = -500, neg False → -500. WRONG sign convention? Actually -500 cents = -$5.00 — the value is coincidentally right but through whole=\"-5\"! Wait: whole=\"-5\", frac=\"00\" → int(\"-5\")*100 + 0 = -500. neg=False → -500. So \"$-5\" → -500 which is correct by accident. But \"$-5.10\" → whole=\"-5\", frac=\"10\" → -500+10 = -490 → WRONG (should be -510). Not documented though. Should I fix? The docstring documents '-$3.10' as THE negative form. Hidden tests check \"documented behaviour\" — \"-$3.10\" form. I'll consider whether to harden. The task says \"Fix the root causes; extra hidden tests will check the documented behaviour beyond the visible tests.\"\n\nI think the intent is: 3-4 planted bugs. Let me verify by running tests and see which fail. The planted bugs are likely:\n1. parse_date m/d swap.\n2. format_cents negative handling.\n3. top_items ascending + no tie-break (or maybe just ascending).\n\nMaybe also something in parse_amount? Let me test parse_amount(\"0.5\") — should be 50 per docstring. Trace: s=\"0.5\", no dot check: \".\" in s → whole=\"0\", frac=\"5\" → (frac+\"00\")[:2] = \"500\"[:2] = \"50\" → 0*100+50 = 50 ✓.\n\nHmm what about \"0.55\"? frac=\"55\" → \"5500\"[:2] = \"55\" ✓.\n\nWhat about \"$1,234.5\"? → 123450 ✓.\n\nHmm, what about int(frac) where frac=\"50\" → 50 ✓.\n\nOK. And negative: \"-$3.10\" → -310 ✓.\n\nHmm wait, actually, hold on. Let me re-examine: is there a bug with negative amounts and the fraction? \"-$3.10\" → neg=True → -310 ✓. \"-$3.99\" → -399 ✓. OK.\n\nHmm, but wait — what about negative with comma \"-$1,234.56\" → -123456 ✓.\n\nOK so parse_amount looks fine. But let me be extra careful about one thing: `s.lstrip(\"-\").lstrip(\"$\")` — what if input is \"-$3.10\" with a space like \"- $3.10\"? Not documented.\n\nNow, hidden tests \"beyond the visible tests\" — they check documented behavior. What documented behaviors aren't covered by visible tests?\n\n1. format_cents docstring: negative values look like '-$0.05' → format_cents(-5) == \"-$0.05\". With my fix: sign=\"-\", abs(5)=5 → 5//100=0, 5%100=5 → \"$0.05\" → \"-$0.05\" ✓.\n\n2. parse_amount docstring: '0.5' → 50 ✓ (already works).\n\n3. parse_date with different dates, e.g., \"2026-01-02\" → (2026, 1, 2). Fixed.\n\n4. parse_record: item stripped + lowercased — e.g., \"2026-10-06 |  Gadget | 1 | $4.00\" → \"gadget\". Works.\n\n5. parse_ledger: blank lines and # lines skipped — visible test covers via SAMPLE.\n\n6. top_items ties: e.g., records with two items tied → name ascending. Fixed with (-total, item) key.\n\n7. summary_line default n=3.\n\n8. totals_by_item sums across multiple records of same item ✓.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me also check `summary_line` for negative totals — format_cents handles after fix.\n\nOne more possible planted bug I should double check: parse_ledger's skip condition. `line.lstrip().startswith(\"#\")` — correct. But hmm, what about `if not line.strip()` — blank ✓.\n\nAnd parse_record's split: should it use `line.split(\"|\")` — what if the line has a trailing \"|\" or extra fields? Not documented.\n\nOK wait, actually, let me reconsider whether there's a bug in parse_record regarding stripping the ITEM only... The docstring says \"Item names are lower-cased and stripped.\" The comprehension strips all parts including item ✓. But what if the item contains internal spaces like \"Super Widget\"? → \"super widget\" ✓ preserved.\n\nAlright, let me also double-check report.summary_line's expected output for the sample: totals widget=1350, gadget=400, gizmo=250. Top 3: widget, gadget, gizmo. \"widget=$13.50; gadget=$4.00; gizmo=$2.50\" ✓.\n\nNow let me run the failing tests to confirm, then fix.\n\nAlso — should I worry about `int(frac)` for something like frac=\"05\" → int(\"05\") = 5 ✓.\n\nOne more thing in parse_amount: `if \".\" in s:` — what if multiple dots \"1.2.3\"? split gives 3 parts, unpack error. Not documented.\n\nLet me now also think about whether `neg` handling should be `s.lstrip(\"-\")` vs removing just one \"-\". \"--5\" is pathological; not documented.\n\nOK, let me also scan for any other files (hidden modules?) — no, just money.py, parse.py, report.py, __init__.py.\n\nLet me run the test suite now.\n\nThen apply the three fixes. Actually, let me think once more about whether format_cents has ANOTHER subtle bug: thousands separator with `,` — format_cents(1234567) → \"$12,345.67\" ✓.\n\nAnd for zero: format_cents(0) → \"$0.00\" ✓.\n\nHmm, and what about the docstring example '-$0.05' → after fix ✓.\n\nNow the parse_date fix: also consider whitespace: \" 2026-10-06 \" → strip ✓.\n\nWhat about \"2026-10-6\" (single-digit day)? int(\"6\") = 6 ✓. Docstring says 'YYYY-MM-DD' but int handles single digits fine.\n\ntop_items fix details:\n\n```python\nranked = sorted(totals_by_item(records).items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))\nreturn ranked[:n]\n```\n\nCheck visible test: totals {widget:1350, gadget:400, gizmo:250} → sorted: widget(-1350), gadget(-400), gizmo(-250) → [widget, gadget][:2] ✓.\n\nTies: {b:400, a:400, c:100} → sorted by (-400,'a'), (-400,'b'), (-100,'c') → [a, b] for n=2 ✓ name ascending.\n\nAlso n larger than items → returns all ✓. n=0 → [] ✓.\n\nNow let me run tests before and after.\n\nAlso, I want to make sure there are no other root causes hiding. Let me diff mentally each function against docstring once more...\n\nparse_amount — hmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider one more time. What if hidden test does parse_amount(\"$0.5\") == 50 ✓. parse_amount(\"-12\") == -1200 ✓. \n\nHmm, but actually there's one more potential issue: `int(whole or \"0\")` — whole=\"0001\" → int → 1 ✓.\n\nWhat about whole with spaces after comma removal, e.g. \"1 2